============================================================
GIT & GITHUB
VS CODE GIT INTEGRATION
============================================================

1. INTRODUCTION

1.1 Git in VS Code

Visual Studio Code (VS Code) has built-in Git integration.

This means you can perform most common Git operations directly from the VS Code interface without using terminal commands.

You can:
- View modified files.
- Track code changes.
- Stage files.
- Create commits.
- Push changes to GitHub.
- Pull updates from GitHub.
- Compare different versions of files.

Instead of executing commands manually, you can perform these operations using buttons and menus.

============================================================
2. UNDERSTANDING THE SOURCE CONTROL PANEL
============================================================

2.1 What is the Source Control Panel?

The Source Control panel is the Git interface available inside VS Code.

You can find it in the left sidebar.

The Source Control icon looks like a branch.

2.2 What Does the Source Control Panel Do?

It provides the following features:

- Displays all modified files in your project.
- Shows added, modified, and deleted files.
- Allows you to stage changes.
- Allows you to create commits.
- Provides options to push and pull changes.
- Helps you review changes before committing.

To open it:

1. Open VS Code.
2. Look at the left sidebar.
3. Click the Source Control icon.

You will see your changed files under the "Changes" section.

============================================================
3. BASIC GIT WORKFLOW USING VS CODE
============================================================

You can perform the basic Git workflow using four simple steps.

3.1 Step 1: View Your Changes

After modifying files in your project:

1. Open the Source Control panel.
2. Look under the "Changes" section.
3. All modified and untracked files will appear here.

To view what changed:

1. Click any file listed under "Changes".
2. VS Code opens a comparison view.
3. Review the differences between the previous version and the current version.

Common visual indicators:

- Green: Added lines.
- Red: Removed lines.

These colors help you identify exactly what changed in your code.

3.2 Step 2: Stage Your Changes

Before creating a commit, you need to stage the files.

Staging means selecting which changes should be included in the next commit.

Method 1: Stage an Individual File

1. Hover over a file under "Changes".
2. Click the "+" icon.
3. The file moves to the "Staged Changes" section.

Method 2: Stage All Files

1. Find the "Changes" section.
2. Hover over its heading.
3. Click the "+" icon next to "Changes".

This stages all eligible changes.

3.3 Step 3: Commit Your Changes

After staging your files:

1. Locate the commit message text box in the Source Control panel.
2. Enter a meaningful commit message.

Example:

    Add login functionality

3. Create the commit by either:
   - Clicking the checkmark (Commit) button.
   - Pressing Ctrl + Enter on Windows/Linux.
   - Pressing Cmd + Enter on macOS.

This creates a Git commit containing the staged changes.

IMPORTANT:
Only staged changes are included in the commit.

3.4 Step 4: Push Changes to GitHub

After committing your changes, you need to upload them to GitHub.

Method 1: Using the Source Control Menu

1. Open the Source Control panel.
2. Click the three-dot menu (...).
3. Select "Push".

Method 2: Using the Status Bar

1. Locate the Sync Changes icon in the bottom status bar.
2. Click it to synchronize your local branch with its remote branch.

This may perform both pull and push operations.

IMPORTANT:
Before pushing, make sure your local repository is connected to the correct GitHub remote repository.

