7. TIPS FOR BEGINNERS
============================================================

7.1 Commit Frequently

Create commits regularly instead of waiting until the entire project is completed.

Benefits:
- Maintains a clear development history.
- Makes it easier to identify changes.
- Helps you return to previous versions.

7.2 Write Clear Commit Messages

Always use meaningful commit messages.

Good examples:

    Fix login bug
    Add user authentication
    Update database configuration
    Improve API response handling

Avoid unclear messages such as:

    changes
    update
    final
    test

A good commit message should explain what was changed.

7.3 Pull Before Starting Work

Before starting work on an existing shared project, pull the latest changes from GitHub.

This helps ensure that you are working with the latest version of the repository.

Command:

    git pull

You can also use the Sync Changes option in VS Code.

7.4 Never Commit Sensitive Information

Never upload sensitive information to GitHub.

Examples:

- API keys.
- Passwords.
- Access tokens.
- Database credentials.
- Environment configuration files.

Always add sensitive files to your .gitignore file.

Example:

    .env
    .env.*
    .venv/

IMPORTANT:
Never commit API keys or .env files to GitHub, even when the repository is private.

If a secret has already been pushed, removing the file in a later commit does not make the exposed secret safe. Revoke or rotate the secret immediately.

============================================================
8. TERMINAL VS VS CODE UI
============================================================

Both approaches allow you to perform Git operations.

You can choose whichever method feels more comfortable.

Comparison:

TASK                  TERMINAL             VS CODE UI
------------------------------------------------------------
Stage files           git add .            Click + icon
Commit changes        git commit -m        Enter message
                      "message"            + Commit
Push changes          git push             Click Push
Pull changes          git pull             Click Pull
Sync changes          git pull + push      Click Sync
Initialize repo       git init             Initialize Repository
Clone repository      git clone            Git: Clone

8.1 Which Method Should You Use?

Terminal:
- Faster for experienced developers.
- Useful for automation and scripting.
- Provides access to advanced Git commands.

VS Code UI:
- Easier for beginners.
- Provides visual feedback.
- Makes reviewing code changes easier.
- Reduces the need to remember commands.

RECOMMENDATION:

Learn both methods.

Start with the VS Code interface to understand the Git workflow visually. Gradually practice the equivalent terminal commands.

